
<p>Steven Levy at Wired, in a terrific post-event follow-up to his exclusive early look at the new MacBook Pros (<a href="https://apple.news/AzfuovlZARN2bha2V8pcsNA">News+ link</a>):</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>I first met Ternus in 2015, when I spent a day with him at Apple’s
Input Design Lab, a testing facility in Cupertino. He’d risen to
VP for Mac, iPad, Ecosystem, and Audio Engineering, and he struck
me as affable and knowledgeable. (He spent four years right out of
college at a virtual reality company, but he’s been at Apple ever
since.) The peg to my story at the time was how this unknown
manager conducted a Javert-like investigation to fix the weird
sound a redesigned mouse made when sliding on a table.</p>

<p>That attention to detail was a legacy of Jobs’ perfectionism,
which Tim Cook sustained as the next CEO. In the decade when
Ternus overlapped with Jobs, he says he had only “a handful of
interactions” with the founder. “But I’ve always felt like sort of
a student of Steve’s teachings,” Ternus said. “And then being able
to work very closely with Tim, I’ve been very lucky.” [...]</p>

<p>The extent to which Ternus is a deep Apple insider became clear
when I probed him on his views on being a corporate leader. I
asked him which non-Apple CEOs he admires, and he was stumped. “I
haven’t really thought about that,” he said. I asked whether any
business books had an impact on him. “Not really,” he said. “Of
course I’ve read books, but there’s so much to be learned from the
people I get to work with.” Indeed, every time I tried to pin down
Ternus on what mark he wants to make at Apple, he deflected the
question and told me how he will continue Apple’s commitment to
internal collaboration.</p>
</blockquote>

